    18	## [1.4.1] - 2026-09-18
    19	
    20	Patch release for adopters who run long autonomous `Workflow` pipelines
    21	(PLAN-190). Every `Workflow` launch is now recorded BEFORE dispatch, a resume
    22	whose `args` differ from the recorded launch is blocked instead of going
    23	through, and the exact recorded call can be read back from the ledger rather
    24	than rebuilt from memory. Both kill-switches are named below. No speed claim.
    25	
    26	### Added
    27	- **Workflow launch ledger + resume guard** (PLAN-190 W1). A new hook,
    28	  `check_workflow_launch.py`, registered on PreToolUse and PostToolUse for the
    29	  `Workflow` tool in the dogfood settings and in the base template (the `user`
    30	  profile keeps it), records every launch BEFORE dispatch — script sha256 plus a
    31	  snapshot of the script bytes, the literal `args` (absent ≠ null), the
    32	  `resumeFromRunId`, the code revision of the working directory — and binds the
    33	  run id the runner returns. A resume over DIFFERENT args is blocked with a
    34	  counts-only reason (the detail stays in the manifest); a resume over a
    35	  different script with the same args is advisory by default
    36	  (`CEO_WORKFLOW_SCRIPT_GUARD=enforce` to block). A block needs a strong bind
    37	  (by `tool_use_id` or manual): a heuristic bind never sustains one, args or
    38	  script alike; a blocked attempt is never a candidate for heuristic binding.
    39	  The recorded manifest is validated before it is evidence (args
    40	  canonical/hash consistency, script hash against its snapshot bytes, id
    41	  shapes): an inconsistent record is inconclusive for the guard and refused
    42	  as "exact" by `relaunch`. ONE override path for every block, carried by the
    43	  call or the process and never by stored state: a `description` starting
    44	  with `CEO_WORKFLOW_RESUME_FORCE: <why>` on the call itself, or
    45	  `CEO_WORKFLOW_RESUME_FORCE=1` in the harness environment — recorded as
    46	  `mismatch_forced` with its source and reason, announced. A `scriptPath`
    47	  is read only as a regular file of at most 8 MiB (FIFO, device, larger
    48	  file or NUL byte ⇒ recorded unreadable, args still compared); a torn
    49	  index line never swallows the next record; the CLI finds the hook's
    50	  ledger from any subdirectory of the project; the plugin build ships the
    51	  recovery CLI with the guard. `args` are recorded in their original key
    52	  order too (`relaunch` prints that order; a reordered resume counts as a
    53	  difference); notices for allowed calls reach the model as
    54	  `additionalContext`; the block reason names the deliberate-change route
    55	  (`args.resume` style resumes declare themselves); the run id is taken from
    56	  a top-level `runId`/`run_id` key of the response, else from the harness
    57	  `Run ID:` label, else from the only id-shaped token (two different
    58	  labelled ids, or two different unlabelled ones, bind nothing); a record
    59	  whose construction or write fails is still inconclusive-recorded or
    60	  breadcrumbed; `relaunch --out` never overwrites.
    61	  `CEO_WORKFLOW_RESUME_GUARD=0` (advisory mode, ledger kept),
    62	  `CEO_WORKFLOW_LEDGER=0` (off). Fail-open on infrastructure, with the gaps
    63	  listed under Known-open below. CLI
    64	  `ceo-launches.py` (`list · show · relaunch · check · bind · orphans ·
    65	  report`) and operator doc `docs/workflow-recovery.md`. Inventory:
    66	  60 hook scripts, 49 wired, 52 event registrations, 72 `_lib` modules.
    67	- **Recovery and approval tools for autonomous pipelines** (PLAN-190 W2/W3).
    68	  Five stdlib-only CLIs under `.claude/scripts/`, called by a workflow phase,
    69	  a recovery rite or a human — NO hook enforces any of them yet (the
    70	  single-writer hook and quota admission are future ceremonies).
    71	  `approval_gate.py` decides APPROVED/REJECTED by code from a policy file
    72	  (minimum score, a closed severity enum, blocking severities, a cap per
    73	  severity, reviewed revision == final revision, green gate): under the
    74	  source repository's default policy
    75	  (`tests/fixtures/approval/policy-default.json`) a missing score, findings
    76	  list, reviewed revision or gate list, a prose score, an out-of-enum
    77	  severity and a wrong scale are REJECTED with the rule named (doc:
    78	  `docs/approval-gate.md`); the inputs that still come out APPROVED are
    79	  listed under Known-open below.
    80	  `test_refs.py`
    81	  normalises test references — file path, node id, bare function,
    82	  `Class.method` — to pytest node ids by static `ast` (tests are never
    83	  executed); an ambiguous BARE name is an error that lists the candidates.
    84	  `mutant_sandbox.py` runs a mutant inside a disposable detached
    85	  worktree of an identified revision, records the outcome per (revision,
    86	  mutant, command) so a valid verification can be reused, removes the copy in
    87	  `finally`, and reports an ERROR outcome if `git status` of the
    88	  implementation tree differs before and after. `worktree_lock.py` gives one
    89	  writer per worktree among callers that use it (`O_EXCL` lock file,
    90	  heartbeat, `steal` when the holder's heartbeat is older than the
    91	  `--stale-minutes` the CALLER passes — the holder's own TTL is not
    92	  consulted — and `release` by the owner or with `--force`).
    93	  `phase_checkpoint.py` keeps an append-only ledger of steps
    94	  completed inside a phase, bound to the code revision: steps recorded under
    95	  another revision are listed as stale and never count.
    96	
    97	### Fixed
    98	- **`ceo-launches.py relaunch --out` could leave a truncated copy and report
    99	  success** (PLAN-190 W1.1). The snapshot copy was written with a single
   100	  `os.write`, which may write fewer bytes than asked — and the truncated file
   101	  is exactly what the recovery rite passes back as `scriptPath`. The write
   102	  now continues until every byte is down; on a HANDLED failure (an I/O error,
   103	  a write that makes no progress, an error surfacing at `close`) the command
   104	  returns rc 2 naming the incomplete write and TRIES to remove the partial
   105	  file: it compares the destination's inode with the one it created (`lstat`)
   106	  and then unlinks by name. Those are two steps, not one atomic operation: a
   107	  concurrent writer that replaces the destination in between loses ITS file,
   108	  and the message still says the partial file was removed. If the `lstat` or
   109	  the `unlink` fails, the partial file STAYS and the message says so. Not
   110	  handled at all: an interruption that is not an `OSError` (Ctrl-C, a signal)
   111	  in the middle of the write can leave a partial file, with no message. So
   112	  this is NOT yet "the whole file or no file", and the cleanup is NOT
   113	  guaranteed to touch only this call's file (known-open below; the structural
   114	  cure — exclusive temporary file, publish by no-replace `link`, never unlink
   115	  the destination — comes after this release).
   116	
   117	### Known-open (found by this release's cross-review)
   118	Cross-review round 1 of the rc.1 candidate (2026-09-18) returned NO-GO on
   119	five declared conditions that were false against the code; no P0. Round 2,
   120	over the same code with the text corrected, returned GO-WITH-CONDITIONS on
   121	two parts and NO-GO on one sentence (the cleanup promise of `relaunch
   122	--out`, above); no P0. Round 3 is again over the same code. Every CODE
   123	finding of rounds 1 and 2 is still open in this release; the verdicts are
   124	under `.claude/plans/PLAN-192/repass-rc1-20260918-NOGO-r1/` and `-r2/`. By
   125	class:
   126	- **Workflow guard** (`launch_ledger.py`). An incomplete read of the index
   127	  (an I/O error midway, or a torn newest line) is not signalled: the guard
   128	  then compares against the previous surviving binding and can BLOCK a
   129	  legitimate resume (exit: the `CEO_WORKFLOW_RESUME_FORCE` declaration). A
   130	  resume from another worktree, or of a run launched before the hook
   131	  existed, finds no manifest and proceeds with NO notice. When both `runId`
   132	  and `run_id` are present and differ, the first wins; an id whose tail is
   133	  too long is bound by its prefix instead of being rejected. When a
   134	  mismatch is NOT blocked (advisory mode, or a heuristic bind) the notice
   135	  names the CURRENT call's manifest as "the recorded call", and once that
   136	  call is bound `relaunch <run-id>` prints the changed inputs. Ledger writes
   137	  follow a symlinked `launches/` directory or
   138	  index file (same-user tampering is outside the threat model). An INLINE
   139	  script larger than 8 MiB is recorded, but its snapshot is read back through
   140	  the 8 MiB reader, so the record is inconclusive for the guard.
   141	- **`relaunch --out`**: the partial-file cases and the non-atomic cleanup
   142	  named under Fixed above; and when the second read of the snapshot fails the
   143	  command prints the "exact recorded call" heading, creates no file and exits
   144	  rc 0 — the copy exists only when the output says "snapshot copied to".
   145	- **`approval_gate.py`** still APPROVES a non-finite score (`"NaN"`,
   146	  `"nan/10"`), a gate entry with no `failed` count or no `cmd` (or a negative
   147	  `failed`), and a policy whose restriction key is misspelled (unknown keys
   148	  are ignored, so the restriction silently disappears). A DUPLICATE JSON key
   149	  in the evidence keeps the last value (`"findings":[…P0…],"findings":[]`
   150	  approves). No default policy file is delivered to adopters: the one the doc
   151	  names is a fixture under `tests/` in the source repository.
   152	- **`test_refs.py`**: a node id whose class does not exist resolves to the
   153	  only test with that function name in the file; parametrised ids
   154	  (`test_x[case]`) are not normalised and come back `node-not-found`; a test
   155	  file that cannot be read or parsed is treated as having no tests, so a name
   156	  that is really ambiguous can resolve.
   157	- **`worktree_lock.py`**: `steal` trusts the caller's `--stale-minutes`, not
   158	  the holder's TTL; two concurrent `steal` calls can both succeed; the fixed
   159	  `writer.lock.tmp` name follows a symlink; `release --force` on an
   160	  unreadable lock is not logged.
   161	- **`mutant_sandbox.py`**: the patch is hashed and then re-read by path
   162	  (another producer can swap it in between); `gc` removes a sandbox that is
   163	  still running (its state stays `creating` until the end); every non-zero
   164	  exit code is recorded as `killed`, including a runner or collection error;
   165	  a command that times out has its direct child killed, but descendants it
   166	  started keep running while the sandbox is removed under them.
   167	- **`phase_checkpoint.py`**: `--rev HEAD` is stored literally; an append
   168	  after a torn line loses both records.
   169	- **Delivery**: `upgrade.sh` delivers `.claude/scripts/local/` (maintainer
   170	  tooling, the release driver `release.sh` included) to adopters, while a
   171	  fresh install does not; no delivered hook, command, settings file,
   172	  template, skill or top-level script references `release.sh`.
   173	
   174	### Known-open (carried from v1.4.0 — NOT cured by this release)
   175	- The signed v1.4.0 verdict carries an annex of P1 findings and declared
   176	  conditions marked "cure in 1.4.1". **This release does not cure them.**
